description:
In repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/feature-compose.ts, REPLACE the following SEVEN consecutive lines with the block given below. This is a REPLACEMENT, not an insertion: the original seven lines must not survive anywhere in the file, and `const lessonReason = String(` must appear EXACTLY ONCE afterwards.

REPLACE these seven consecutive lines, verbatim:
    const lessonReason = String(
      failedApply?.detail
      ?? failedVerify?.output
      ?? semantic_gate?.reason
      ?? verdict,
    );
    await appendComposeLesson(lessonClass, lessonReason, [...touched].join(","), pointer.gap);

WITH these lines, verbatim:
    // DISTIL THE VERIFY OUTPUT (2026-09-11). `failedVerify.output` is the WHOLE build
    // log and the stored reason is truncated to its first ~200 characters — which is the
    // install preamble ("== install ==", "DRYRUN_EXIT=0", "@types/bun@1.3.14"), never the
    // failure. Measured: 401 verify_failed rows in the corpus, 123 beginning with that
    // preamble, and ZERO naming the test or error that actually failed. The corpus held
    // 401 records of verification failure that could not say what failed, and the same
    // head-slice reached the drafter's prompt. Keep the lines that carry the diagnosis.
    const verifyLines = failedVerify
      ? failedVerify.output.split("\n").filter((l) => /error TS\d|error:|\(fail\)|expect\(/.test(l)).slice(0, 6).join(" | ")
      : "";
    const lessonReason = String(
      failedApply?.detail
      ?? (verifyLines || failedVerify?.output)
      ?? semantic_gate?.reason
      ?? verdict,
    );
    await appendComposeLesson(lessonClass, lessonReason, [...touched].join(","), pointer.gap);

Only the middle of the `String(...)` expression changes — `?? failedVerify?.output` becomes `?? (verifyLines || failedVerify?.output)` — plus a new `verifyLines` declaration above it. The `await appendComposeLesson(...)` line is reproduced UNCHANGED and must be kept, so that the use of `lessonReason` stays in the same edit as its declaration.

ANCHORING: `const lessonReason = String(` occurs EXACTLY ONCE in this file, as does the `await appendComposeLesson(lessonClass, lessonReason, ...)` call.

WHY. When a compose fails verification, `failedVerify.output` is the ENTIRE build log and is stored as the lesson's reason. The stored reason is truncated to roughly its first 200 characters, and the first 200 characters of a build log are the dependency-install preamble, not the failure.

MEASURED 2026-09-11 over the live corpus (/workspace/proposals/compose-lessons.jsonl):
  verify_failed rows                                    401
  ...naming the test or error that actually failed        0
  ...whose reason STARTS with the install preamble      123
  median reason length                             200 chars
The corpus holds 401 records of verification failure and cannot say what failed. A real example of a stored reason, in full: '== install == == resolve == DRYRUN_EXIT=0 @types/bun@1.3.14 typescript@5.9.3 bun-types@1.3.14 @types/node@25.9.1 undici-types@7.24.6 [1.00ms] done == typecheck == $ tsc --noEmit src/resolvers/lea'. It ends mid-word, before reaching a single error line.

THIS HAS TWO CONSUMERS AND BOTH ARE DEGRADED. The corpus is the substrate's record of why composes fail. It is also mirrored into the drafter's prompt, where these same entries now render as one line each of install preamble — occupying a slot that was added specifically to show the drafter a real verdict. One defect, two symptoms.

It also caused a misdiagnosis: searching the corpus for a failing test name returned zero hits, which was read as the failure being rare. The failure was recorded 401 times; the search could not find it because the stored text never contains it.

THE FIX. Keep only the lines carrying the diagnosis — anything matching `error TS<digit>`, `error:`, `(fail)` or `expect(` — capped at the first six, joined with ' | '. These are the lines a human reads first when a build fails.

SAFETY. If no line matches, `verifyLines` is the empty string and `verifyLines || failedVerify?.output` falls back to the full output exactly as today. If there was no verify failure at all, `verifyLines` is the empty string and the `??` chain proceeds to `semantic_gate?.reason` and then `verdict`, unchanged — note the `(verifyLines || failedVerify?.output)` parenthesis yields `undefined` in that case because `failedVerify` is undefined, so `??` moves on exactly as before. The apply-failure branch above is untouched and still takes precedence. No signature, no type, and no other call site changes.

